.TH EUCLID-CLI-EAG-UPDATE-ROUTE 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.53" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-eag-update-route \- change an existing gateway route
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli eag update-route
.BR \-\-route\-id | \-r
.I ID
.RB [ \-\-path | \-p
.IR PREFIX ]
.RB [ \-\-type | \-t
.IR proxy | upload ]
.RB [ \-\-application | \-a
.IR NAME ]
.RB [ \-\-module | \-M
.IR NAME ]
.RB [ \-\-action
.IR NAME ]
.RB [ \-\-bucket | \-b
.IR ERN ]
.RB [ \-\-key\-prefix | \-k
.IR PREFIX ]
.RB [ \-\-max\-bytes | \-x
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-part\-size | \-s
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-content\-types | \-c
.IR LIST ]
.RB [ \-\-region | \-R
.IR NAME ]
.RB [ \-\-namespace | \-N
.IR NAME ]
.RB [ \-\-methods | \-m
.IR LIST ]
.RB [ \-\-authentication | \-A
.IR none | euclid | basic ]
.RB [ \-\-timeout | \-T
.IR SECONDS ]
.RB [ \-\-active
.IR true | false ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Changes an existing route. Anything not named is left as it is, so one
setting can be changed without restating the rest. The result is checked
as a whole: an upload route must still name a bucket and no application,
and must still require authentication, however few of those this command
changed.
.PP
The gateway re-reads its routes every
.I euclid.modules.eag.refresh\-seconds
(5 by default), so a change takes effect shortly after this returns
rather than at the next restart.
.SH TAKING A PATH OUT OF SERVICE
.B \-\-active false
is the way to stop serving a path without losing how it was configured.
The gateway stops carrying it and answers 404, and
.B \-\-active true
puts it back exactly as it was \- which is what a maintenance window
wants, and what
.BR euclid-cli-eag-delete-route (1)
cannot offer.
.PP
An inactive route is still listed by
.BR euclid-cli-eag-list-routes (1),
because it is configuration that exists but is not being served.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-route\-id " " \fIID\fR
Route to change. Required.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-path " " \fIPREFIX\fR
New path prefix. Must start with \fI/\fR, and must not be a path another
route already claims.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-application " " \fINAME\fR
Application the requests are sent to. Must already exist. Changing this
moves the path to another application without any URL changing, since
the path is forwarded unchanged either way.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-type " " \fIproxy\fR|\fIupload\fR
Change what kind of route this is; see
.BR euclid-cli-eag-create-route (1).
.TP
.BR \-M ", " \-\-module " " \fINAME\fR
Euclid module the requests are sent to instead of an application.
.TP
.B \-\-action " " \fINAME\fR
The one action the module answers for on this route.
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-bucket " " \fIERN\fR
Upload routes only. ERN of the bucket bodies are written to.
.TP
.BR \-k ", " \-\-key\-prefix " " \fIPREFIX\fR
Upload routes only. Prefix every key is confined to.
.TP
.BR \-x ", " \-\-max\-bytes " " \fIN\fR
Upload routes only. Largest body accepted, in bytes;
.I 0
for no limit.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-part\-size " " \fIN\fR
Upload routes only. Bytes per part streamed to ESM.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-content\-types " " \fILIST\fR
Upload routes only. Comma-separated content types accepted; an empty
string for any.
.TP
.BR \-R ", " \-\-region " " \fINAME\fR
Region requests on this route act in.
.TP
.BR \-N ", " \-\-namespace " " \fINAME\fR
Namespace requests on this route act in.
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-methods " " \fILIST\fR
Comma-separated HTTP methods. Replaces the whole list rather than adding
to it, so
.B "\-\-methods GET"
leaves a route answering for GET alone. An empty string
.RB ( "\-\-methods ''" )
puts it back to answering for every method.
.RS
.PP
Must not overlap with another route on the same path; see
.BR euclid-cli-eag-create-route (1).
.RE
.TP
.BR \-A ", " \-\-authentication " " \fInone\fR|\fIeuclid\fR|\fIbasic\fR
What the gateway requires of a caller. See
.BR euclid-cli-eag-create-route (1).
.TP
.BR \-T ", " \-\-timeout " " \fISECONDS\fR
How long the backend has to answer a request on this route, 1 to 3600;
.B 0
puts the route back on the gateway's default,
.IR euclid.gateway.http.backend\-timeout .
See
.BR euclid-cli-eag-create-route (1).
.TP
.BR \-\-active " " \fItrue\fR|\fIfalse\fR
Whether the route is served.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The route was updated. Its new definition is printed as JSON.
.TP
.B 1
Missing or invalid arguments, a path that does not start with \fI/\fR, a
path and method another route already claims, an unrecognised
.B \-\-authentication
or
.BR \-\-methods ,
no administrator session, a route or application that does not exist, or
the server rejected the request.
.SH EXAMPLES
Require a euclid credential for a route that was public:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli eag update-route \e
    --route-id resource \e
    --authentication euclid
.fi
.RE
.PP
Take a path out of service for a deployment, then put it back:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli eag update-route --route-id resource --active false
euclid-cli eag update-route --route-id resource --active true
.fi
.RE
.PP
Narrow a route to reads only, then open it up again:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli eag update-route --route-id resource --methods GET,HEAD
euclid-cli eag update-route --route-id resource --methods ''
.fi
.RE
.PP
Move a path to a replacement application:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli eag update-route \e
    --route-id resource \e
    --application onix-parser-v2
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-eag (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eag-create-route (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eag-get-route (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eag-delete-route (1)
